{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Knot.GluingBridge","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Knot.GluingBridge","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Knot.FlatTetrahedra"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoil_satisfiesEdgeEquations","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoil_satisfiesEdgeEquations","kind":"theorem","line":189,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L189-L203","doc":"**The trefoil triangulation satisfies its edge gluing equations**, as an inhabitant of\n`ShapedTriangulation.SatisfiesEdgeEquations` rather than as loose identities.\n\nThis is what `glhi` asks for: the generated identities stop being standalone and start\n*certifying* a structure the rest of the corpus can consume.  The products are unfolded by\n`Fin.prod_univ_two` / `Fin.prod_univ_three` and discharged by the two theorems above. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoil_edge_one_product","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoil_edge_one_product","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L179-L187","doc":"**The trefoil satisfies its second edge equation in product form**: `z₀ · z₁′ = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoil_edge_zero_product","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoil_edge_zero_product","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L165-L177","doc":"**The trefoil satisfies its first edge equation in product form.**\n\nDerived from `satisfies_of_cleared_two` — no `field_simp`, no cofactor, just the cleared\nidentity plus non-vanishing.  Both shapes are real, so this is a statement about a\ngenuinely degenerate solution, which is the point of the flat-tolerant structure. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoilGluing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.trefoilGluing","kind":"def","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L160-L163","doc":"The trefoil's edge exponent data, from SnapPy: rows `[1,2,2, 2,1,2]` and\n`[1,0,0, 0,1,0]`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfiesEdgeEquations_of_cleared","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfiesEdgeEquations_of_cleared","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L142-L149","doc":"**The whole edge system**, from the cleared identity at every edge. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfies_edge_of_cleared","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfies_edge_of_cleared","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L120-L140","doc":"**Cleared form ⟹ `SatisfiesEdgeEquations`, for one edge, at any `n`.**\n\nThe hypothesis is the cleared identity for edge `k` — exactly the shape\n`GeneratedGluing` proves, once its explicit products are read as `Finset` products.  The\nconclusion is one conjunct of `SatisfiesEdgeEquations`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfies_of_cleared_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfies_of_cleared_two","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L101-L111","doc":"**Cleared form ⟹ product form**, for two tetrahedra.\n\nNote the hypothesis multiplies the two numerators and the two denominators — which is\nprecisely how the generated theorems state a multi-tetrahedron edge equation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfies_of_cleared_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.satisfies_of_cleared_one","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L85-L93","doc":"**Cleared form ⟹ product form**, for one tetrahedron.\n\nThe hypothesis is exactly what `GeneratedGluing` proves; the conclusion is exactly what\n`SatisfiesEdgeEquations` requires of a single factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.Shape.param_pow_den_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.Shape.param_pow_den_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L71-L75","doc":"The denominator appearing in `param_pow_prod` is nonzero — needed to turn a quotient\nequation into an equation of numerators. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Knot.FlatTet.Shape.param_pow_prod","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Knot.FlatTet.Shape.param_pow_prod","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Knot/GluingBridge.lean#L48-L69","doc":"**One tetrahedron's contribution, as a single quotient.**\n\n`z^a · (1/(1−z))^b · (1 − 1/z)^c = z^a (z−1)^c / ((1−z)^b z^c)`.\n\nThis is the whole bridge: it turns the three edge parameters — two of which are\nquotients — into one numerator over one denominator, in a form that composes across\ntetrahedra. ","signatureChunk":0}]}